   1: # Supabase setup (auth and scan history)
   2: 
   3: TrustShield uses one Supabase project for opt-in user accounts and the per-user scan history. It
   4: is new infrastructure, added alongside the existing stateless analysis pipeline â€” anonymous
   5: scanning is completely unaffected and nothing about detection/fusion changed.
   6: 
   7: ## IMPLEMENTED
   8: 
   9: ### Project
  10: 
  11: - Project name: `trustshield-ai`, organization `shubhsaxena2020's Org` (Free plan).
  12: - Region: `ap-south-1` (South Asia, Mumbai) â€” closest available region to the team and most
  13:   users.
  14: - Project ref: `xssorflxyuanvkpukjon`. Project URL: `https://xssorflxyuanvkpukjon.supabase.co`.
  15: - Created 2026-10-11 via browser automation (same approach used for the Google Safe Browsing key
  16:   in an earlier session). The database password is recorded only in `.env`, never in this doc or
  17:   in git.
  18: - This project uses Supabase's current key format (`sb_publishable_...` /
  19:   `sb_secret_...`) rather than the legacy JWT `anon`/`service_role` keys. Both formats work
  20:   identically with the `apikey` + `Authorization: Bearer` header pattern used throughout
  21:   `services/api/auth.py` and `services/history/store.py` â€” nothing in the app code assumes one
  22:   format over the other.
  23: 
  24: ### Schema
  25: 
  26: One table, `scan_history`, created via the SQL editor:
  27: 
  28: ```sql
  29: create table scan_history (
  30:   id uuid primary key default gen_random_uuid(),
  31:   user_id uuid not null references auth.users(id) on delete cascade,
  32:   input_type text not null check (input_type in ('text', 'url', 'image', 'qr')),
  33:   input_summary text not null,
  34:   risk_score int not null,
  35:   risk_label text not null,
  36:   result_json jsonb not null,
  37:   created_at timestamptz not null default now()
  38: );
  39: 
  40: alter table scan_history enable row level security;
  41: 
  42: create policy "owner can select own history"
  43:   on scan_history for select using (auth.uid() = user_id);
  44: 
  45: create policy "owner can delete own history"
  46:   on scan_history for delete using (auth.uid() = user_id);
  47: ```
  48: 
  49: No insert/update policy exists for the `anon`/`authenticated` roles: every write goes through the
  50: backend's service-role (now secret-key) client, which bypasses RLS by design. RLS is the second
  51: line of defense behind the backend's own `user_id` filtering, not the only one â€” see
  52: `docs/AUTH_AND_HISTORY_ARCHITECTURE.md`.
  53: 
  54: ### Auth
  55: 
  56: Supabase Auth, email/password only (no OAuth providers configured) â€” opt-in accounts for history,
  57: never a gate in front of scanning.
  58: 
  59: ### Environment variables
  60: 
  61: Backend (`.env`, git-ignored):
  62: 
  63: ```
  64: SUPABASE_URL=https://xssorflxyuanvkpukjon.supabase.co
  65: SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<the project's secret key>
  66: ```
  67: 
  68: Frontend (`apps/web/.env.local`, git-ignored):
  69: 
  70: ```
  71: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://xssorflxyuanvkpukjon.supabase.co
  72: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<the project's publishable key>
  73: ```
  74: 
  75: Both sets of actual secret values live only in the git-ignored env files, never in this
  76: repository's tracked files.
  77: 
  78: ### Recreating this from scratch
  79: 
  80: 1. Create a new Supabase project (any region), note its URL and keys from Project Settings â†’
  81:    API Keys.
  82: 2. Run the schema SQL above in the SQL editor.
  83: 3. Set the four environment variables above in `.env` and `apps/web/.env.local`.
  84: 4. No further manual steps â€” the backend and frontend read these at startup/build time.
